Instead of writing each value immediately, the helper adds values to a string and prints once at the base case. The recursive calls still visit the numbers from 1 through N.

This avoids a trailing space in the final output. It also makes the zero and negative cases easy to handle.

```javascript
class Solution {
    printNumbers(n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        const buildOutput = (current, output) => {
            if (current > n) {
                console.log(output.trim());
                return;
            }

            buildOutput(current + 1, output + current + " ");
        };

        buildOutput(1, "");
    }
}
```

With `n = 3`, the recursive string changes from empty to `1`, then `1 2`, then `1 2 3`, which is printed when the next value would be 4.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in JavaScript because creating a new string at every step can copy the existing output.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case when counting the stored strings across the recursive calls.

This version lets the recursive call handle all smaller numbers first. When the call returns, `n` is printed, so the output comes out from 1 to N.

Dry run for `n = 3`:
- `printNumbers(3)` calls `printNumbers(2)`.
- `printNumbers(2)` calls `printNumbers(1)`.
- `printNumbers(1)` calls `printNumbers(0)` and then prints `1`.
- The waiting calls then print `2` and `3`.

```java
class Solution {
    public void printNumbers(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        printNumbers(n - 1);
        System.out.print(n + " ");
    }
}
```

The input value is not changed. The method also handles zero and negative values by returning immediately.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because there can be `n` active calls.

The range is split into two smaller ranges. The left half is printed first, then the right half, so the final order is still increasing. This is a different recursive shape from simply moving a counter by one.

The extra base check handles a range with one value. Invalid ranges return without printing.

```go
import "fmt"

func printRange(left int, right int) {
    if left > right {
        return
    }

    if left == right {
        fmt.Print(left, " ")
        return
    }

    middle := left + (right-left)/2
    printRange(left, middle)
    printRange(middle+1, right)
}

func printNumbers(n int) {
    if n <= 0 {
        return
    }

    printRange(1, n)
}
```

For example, `n = 5` may split into `1..3` and `4..5`, while each smaller range is handled in the same way.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each number is printed once, although the recursive tree has extra calls.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the balanced recursion depth.

A simple counter keeps the order clear. Start at 1, print the current number, and call the helper for the next number. The recursion stops after reaching `n`.

For example, with `n = 3`, the calls print `1`, then `2`, then `3`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    void printFrom(int current, int n) {
        if (current > n) {
            return;
        }

        cout << current << " ";
        printFrom(current + 1, n);
    }

  public:
    void printNumbers(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        printFrom(1, n);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because every number is printed once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion call stack. For `n <= 0`, the method prints nothing.

